Hey !

Thank you Murray for your answers always very relevant.
I still block on the choice of the best detection model wondering how I will justify it in an article. As Murray suggested, I ran the models with CL = TRUE and now the AICc indicate that 3 of them should be considered (dAICc < 2).

> AIC(null.CL, sig0.sex.CL <http://sig0.sex.CL>, sig.sex.CL <http://sig.sex.CL>, g0.sex.CL <http://g0.sex.CL>)

                    model               detectfn   npar    logLik     AIC       AICc     dAICc    AICcwt

g0.sex.CL <http://g0.sex.CL>    g0~h2 sigma~1 pmix~h2     halfnormal    4    -415.9066   839.813   841.631   0.000   0.4134
sig.sex.CL <http://sig.sex.CL>   g0~1 sigma~h2 pmix~h2     halfnormal    4    -416.0662   840.132   841.951   0.320   0.3523
sig0.sex.CL <http://sig0.sex.CL> g0~h2 sigma~h2 pmix~h2     halfnormal    5    -415.3412   840.682   843.540   1.909   0.1592
null.CL       g0~1 sigma~1 pmix~h2     halfnormal    3    -418.9999   844.000   845.043   3.412   0.0751

As Murray also suggested, one possibility would be to model average these models. The best density model being with the eastness as covariate, I ran these models with CL = FALSE (if TRUE, almost all the models have a dAICc < 2)  :
D~eastness g0~h2 sigma~1
D~eastness g0~1 sigma~h2
D~eastness g0~h2 sigma~h2
D~eastness g0~1 sigma~1

But now if I compare AICc of these models, I have only the two first ones to consider.
> AIC(sig0.sex, D.hab.sig0, D.east.sig0, D.north.sig0, D.east.g0, D.east.sig, D.east)

                                          model   detectfn npar    logLik     AIC    AICc dAICc AICcwt

D.east.g0      D~eastness g0~h2 sigma~1 pmix~h2 halfnormal    6 -417.8732 847.746 851.946 0.000 0.4225
D.east.sig     D~eastness g0~1 sigma~h2 pmix~h2 halfnormal    6 -418.1320 848.264 852.464 0.518 0.3261
D.east.sig0   D~eastness g0~h2 sigma~h2 pmix~h2 halfnormal    7 -417.2232 848.446 854.341 2.395 0.1276
D.east          D~eastness g0~1 sigma~1 pmix~h2 halfnormal    5 -421.1114 852.223 855.080 3.134 0.0882

There are now only two models with dAICc < 2. Should I stay on the first rank or trust the second ? (1)

Let's imagine I decide to stick to the first one with CL. I model average these models :
D~eastness g0~h2
D~eastness sigma~h2
D~eastness g0~h2 sigma~h2.
But I get this : Error: unable to allocate a vector of size 2.7 GB.
How would you do it ? (2)

///

I wonder if I shouldn’t just follow that logic. The sex is correlated to g0 and sigma and it really makes sense to have bigger territory for males and less chance to detect females (we talk about lynx). It seems unlikely that it is the one or the other, both already proven. So why even consider the two phenomena separately and not just model them at the same time? (3)

So let's imagine now I only consider this model D~eastness g0~h2 sigma~h2. My next question is how to compute one single value of density which varies according to eastness. I find this solution without knowing if it's correct and reliable.
east.lynxS <- predictDsurface(D.east.sig0, se.D = TRUE, cl.D = TRUE)
east.lynxS2013 = as.data.frame(covariates(east.lynxS[1]))
summary(east.lynxS2013)
east.lynxS2015 = as.data.frame(covariates(east.lynxS[2]))
summary(east.lynxS2015)
east.lynxS2018 = as.data.frame(covariates(east.lynxS[3]))
summary(east.lynxS2018)

What do you think about it ? (4)
The results look good with realistic densities and acceptable SE. I can note that that densities slightly vary across sessions but nothing significant, I don't know why.

///

Because I analyze data from a national park at the Albanian and Kosovo frontier, the mask encompasses these countries. The idea of a density value only for the park popped up mainly for conservation reason. Is it relevant ? Is this estimate valable also just for the park ? (5)

I have no exceptations that one person will answer all my questions so don't hesitate to participate. Moreover, I know some of my questions are more ecological than statistical but you might all have good remarks on this too.

Cheers
Yon

