Hi Murray,

Thanks for responding so quickly! Yes, I am aware that the data is very sparse. However, it is part of a larger dataset and I'm doing this because my boss wants me to try analyzing the most problematic part of the dataset as a er... "proof of concept". Anyway, I tried running the following line of code:

m1 <- secr.fit(catCH, mask=catmasks, model=list(D~session, lambda0~1, sigma~1), verify=FALSE, details = list(autoini = 14))

But I got the following error message:

Error in array(x, c(length(x), 1L), if (!is.null(names(x))) list(names(x),  :
  'data' must be of a vector type, was 'NULL'
In addition: Warning message:
In secr.fit(catCH, mask = catmasks, model = list(D ~ session, lambda0 ~  :
  using default starting values

Is this an issue with running secr in R 4.1.2? Or perhaps is it an issue with the detector file format?

Cheers,
Josh

